			<div class="content" id="post-content-2668648">
									

<p>As far as semantic satiation goes, this is more of a Buffalo buffalo than a former library or a body in the water. I appreciate the idea of cross-linking, but those two very strong articles merely show up the weakness of this one.</p>
<p>I'm not convinced by the logic of the anomaly. If every part of this woman, and everything connected with her, is perceived to be her, then it makes no sense to refer to &quot;Allison Eckhart Containment Cells&quot; or &quot;Level Allison ECKHART suits&quot;. If the suits and the cells are perceived to be women, then the idea of wearing them or containing people inside them would be abhorrent, not form part of the containment procedures.</p>
<p>Similarly, later references to &quot;deepest Allison Eckharts&quot; and &quot;100 liters of Allison Eckhart&quot; don't make a huge amount of sense to me - those aren't phrases that one would use to refer to a person, so I don't think the semiotics works.</p>
<p>So unless I'm missing something in the logic, this just becomes another exercise in &quot;skip that affects its own documentation&quot;, of which it is merely a fair example of something already done very well. Downvoted.</p>

							</div>

								
					
